{"records":[{"id":"f52y9zkpzm","title":"RNF31 inhibition sensitizes tumors to bystander killing by innate and adaptive immune cells","description":"Tumor escape mechanisms for immunotherapy include deficiencies in antigen presentation, diminishing adaptive CD8+ T cell antitumor activity. Although innate NK cells are triggered by loss of MHC class I, their response is often inadequate. To increase tumor susceptibility to both innate and adaptive immune elimination, we performed parallel genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens under NK and CD8+ T cell pressure. We identified all components, RNF31, RBCK1, and SHARPIN, of the linear ubiquitination chain assembly complex (LUBAC). Genetic and pharmacologic ablation of RNF31, an E3 ubiquitin ligase, strongly sensitized melanoma, breast and colorectal cancer cells to both NK and CD8+ T cell killing. This occurred in a TNF-dependent manner, causing loss of A20 and non-canonical IKK complexes from TNF Receptor Complex I. Corroborating this preclinically, a small molecule RNF31 inhibitor sensitized human colon carcinoma organoids to TNF and greatly enhanced immune bystander killing of antigen-loss and antigen presentation machinery-deficient tumor cells. These results merit exploration of RNF31 inhibition as a clinical pharmacological opportunity for immunotherapy-refractory cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-30T07:01:54.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f52y9zkpzm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f52y9zkpzm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengkui","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tbk3n9m6y7","title":"Anonymized raw ADCC dataset associated with the manuscript:  \"How past transient viremia shape Natural Killer cells’ antibody-dependent functions in youth with perinatal HIV\"","description":"This dataset contains anonymized participant-level measurements of antibody-dependent NK cell functions used for the analyses presented in the associated manuscript. Each row corresponds to one experimental condition for one participant, identified by an anonymous participant identifier (PID). No direct personal identifiers are included.\nThe dataset includes measurements of NK cell functional responses following plasma-mediated stimulation, including degranulation (CD107a expression), cytokine production (IFN-g), polyfunctional responses and target cell killing activity.\nThe dataset contains the variables required to reproduce the statistical analyses in the main figures of the presented manuscript. Missing values are reported as N/A and all numerical values are reported using decimal points.\nVariable coding:\n•\tCondition: no plasma, HIV-negative plasma (pHD), HIV-positive autologous plasma (pHIVauto), or HIV-positive heterologous plasma (pHIVhetero).\n•\tGender: F = Female; M = Male.\n•\tAge: years at sampling.\n•\tDetectable Viral Load (VL) group: 0 = no previous detectable viremia; 1 = one previous episode of detectable viremia; 2 = two or more previous episodes of detectable viremia.\n•\tADCC functional variables: CD107a+, IFN-g+, CD107a+IFN-g-, CD107a-IFN-g+, CD107a+IFN-g+, and CD107a-IFN-g- NK cell frequencies are reported as percentages of total NK cells.\n•\tNK cell expression variables: CD16 and Granzyme B/Perforin expression levels are reported as geometric mean fluorescence intensity values of NK cells.\n•\tKilling activity: percentage of target cell killing measured in the Infected cell elimination assay.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-28T09:03:05.09Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tbk3n9m6y7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tbk3n9m6y7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Arianna","last_name":"Rotili","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chiara","last_name":"Pighi","orcid":"0000-0001-6163-0135"},{"first_name":"Alessandro","last_name":"Pucci","orcid":"0009-0003-4999-2629"},{"first_name":"nicola","last_name":"cotugno","orcid":"0000-0002-7748-1581"},{"first_name":"Paolo","last_name":"Palma","orcid":"0000-0002-3066-4719"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Flow Cytometry","Demographics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fk84v36dz2","title":"Single-cell transcriptomic analysis of NK cell dynamics in myeloma patients reveal persistent reduction of cytotoxic NK cells from diagnosis to relapse","description":"Single-cell transcriptomics was performed to investigate the bone marrow NK cell compartment of myeloma patients at diagnosis (n=19), during treatment (n=21) and at relapse (n=6). The bone marrow of myeloma patients is characterized by a reduction in conventional cytotoxic NK cells that persists throughout treatment. We show in 20% of newly diagnosed myeloma patients that an altered balance between cytotoxic and cytokine-producing NK cells translates into a reduced cytotoxic ability in response to therapeutic antibodies. The relative loss of cytotoxic NK cells persists at relapse and is accompanied by an expansion of IFN-responsive NK cells. These findings reveal previously unappreciated alterations in bone marrow NK cell composition and highlight the importance of understanding the bone marrow immune system in patients receiving immunotherapies.","source":{"id":"d0034bb1-0408-42d6-bfeb-6f829715ea4d","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-07-11T16:23:25.681Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fk84v36dz2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fk84v36dz2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sabrin ","last_name":"Tahri","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tom","last_name":"Cupedo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Madelon","last_name":"de Jong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Multiple Myeloma","Natural Killer Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ctbbr36dhw","title":"Lack of the IFN-γ signal leads to lethal Orientia tsutsugamushi infection in mice with skin eschar lesions","description":"Scrub typhus is an acute febrile disease due to Orientia tsutsugamushi (Ot) infection and can be life-threatening with organ failure, hemorrhage, and fatality. Yet, little is known as to how the host reacts to Ot bacteria at early stages of infection; no reports have addressed the functional roles of type I versus type II interferon (IFN) responses in scrub typhus. In this study, we used comprehensive intradermal (i.d.) inoculation models and two clinically predominant Ot strains (Karp and Gilliam) to uncover early immune events. Karp infection induced sequential expression of Ifnb and Ifng in inflamed skin and draining lymph nodes at days 1 and 3 post-infection. Using double Ifnar1-/-Ifngr1-/- and Stat1-/- mice, we found that deficiency in IFN/STAT1 signaling resulted in lethal infection with profound pathology and skin eschar lesions, that resembled to human scrub typhus. Further analyses demonstrated that deficiency in IFN-γ, but not IFN-I, resulted in impaired NK cell and macrophage activation and uncontrolled bacterial growth and dissemination, leading to metabolic dysregulation, excessive inflammatory cell infiltration, and exacerbated tissue damage. NK cells were found to be the major cellular source of early IFN-γ, contributing to the initial Ot control. In vitro studies with dendritic cell cultures revealed a superior antibacterial effect offered by IFN-γ than IFN-β. Comparative in vivo studies with Karp- and Gilliam-infection revealed a crucial role of IFN-γ signaling in protection against progression of eschar lesions and Ot infection lethality. Additionally, our i.d. mouse models of lethal infection with eschar lesions are promising tools for immunological study and vaccine development for scrub typhus. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-05T20:27:24.002Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ctbbr36dhw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ctbbr36dhw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yuejin","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI132674"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI156536"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI179997"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI153586"}],"categories":["Interferon","Skin Lesion","Natural Killer Cell","Bacterial Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y59s4xhpzn","title":"Human innate immune cytokine response to dengue virus","description":"Fluorescence minus four controls for flow cytometry experiments of innate immune cytokine production in response  to dengue virus. Pseudo color flow cytometry plots for a representative patient comparing the fluorescence minus four (FMF; top row) control with completely stained sample (bottom) for secretion of (A) TNF-α, (B) IP-10, (C) IL-10, (D) IL-6 and (E) IFN-γ from the indicated cell subsets. Abbreviations: CM – CD14+CD16- classical monocytes; IM – CD14+CD16+ intermediate monocytes; NCM – CD14-CD16+ non-classical monocytes; G – Granulocytes; NKT – CD56+CD3+ natural killer T cells; NK++ - CD56+CD16+ natural killer cells; NK+- - CD56+CD16- natural killer cells; B – CD19+ cells. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-03T07:38:23.625Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y59s4xhpzn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y59s4xhpzn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Vijaya","last_name":"Satchidanandam","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Flow Cytometry","Innate Immunity","Dengue Fever"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bvd647g3mc","title":"Early IFN-a signatures and persistent dysfunction are distinguishing features of NK cells in severe COVID-19","description":"Supplemental tables of the publication \"Early IFN-a signatures and persistent dysfunction are distinguishing features of NK cells in severe COVID-19\" Benjamin Krämer et al., 2021. \n\nSup. Table S1: Cohort details [related to all Figures]\nSup. Table S2: marker for NK selection and NK subtype marker gene list  from the scRNA-seq analyses cohort 1 [related to Figure 2A+D+C and S2A]\nSup. Table S3: Module marker gene list and function analysis from the scRNA-seq analyses cohort 1 [related to Figure 2B]\nSup. Table S4: Module marker gene list, function analysis, TF and ligand prediction from the scRNA-seq analyses cohort 1 [related to Figure 3C]\nSup. Table S5: List of antibodies used for multi-color flow cytometry [related to Figures 1, S1, 2, S2, 3, S3, S4 S6, 7, S7]\nSup. Table S6: Number of donors, samples and cells by experiment [related to all figures]\nSup. Table S7: Oligo-Primer sequences  [related to STAR methods]\n\nBenjamin Krämer, Rainer Knoll, Lorenzo Bonaguro, Michael ToVinh, Jan Raabe, Rosario Astaburuaga-García, Jonas Schulte-Schrepping, Kim Melanie Kaiser, Gereon J. Rieke, Jenny Bischoff, Malte B. Monin, Christoph Hoffmeister, Stefan Schlabe, Elena De Domenico, Nico Reusch, Kristian Händler, Gary Reynolds, Nils Blüthgen, Gudrun Hack, Claudia Finnemann, Hans D. Nischalke, Christian P. Strassburg, Emily Stephenson, Yapeng Su, Louis Gardner, Dan Yuan, Daniel Chen, Jason Goldman, Philipp Rosenstiel, Susanne V. Schmidt, Eicke Latz, Kevin Hrusovsky, Andrew J. Ball, Joe M. Johnson, Paul-Albert Koenig, Florian I. Schmidt, Muzlifah Haniffa, James R. Heath, Beate M. Kümmerer, Verena Keitel, Björn Jensen, Paula Stubbemann, Florian Kurth, Leif E. Sander, Birgit Sawitzki, Deutsche COVID-19 OMICS Initiative (DeCOI), Anna C. Aschenbrenner, Joachim L. Schultze, Jacob Nattermann\nSummary\nLongitudinal analyses of the innate immune system including earliest time points are essential to understand the immunopathogenesis and clinical course of COVID-19. Here, we performed a detailed characterization of natural killer cells in 205 patients (403 samples, day 2-41 after symptom onset) from four independent cohorts using single-cell transcriptomics and proteomics together with functional studies. We found elevated IFN-α plasma levels in early severe COVD-19 alongside increased NK cell expression of ISGs and genes involved in IFN-α signaling, while upregulation of TNF-induced genes was observed in moderate disease. NK cells exert anti-SARS-CoV-2 activity but are functionally impaired in severe COVID-19. Further, NK cell dysfunction may be relevant for development of fibrotic lung disease in severe COVID-19, as NK cells exhibited impaired anti-fibrotic activity. Our study indicates preferential IFN-α and TNF responses in severe and moderate COVID-19, respectively, and associates prolonged IFN-α-induced NK cell response with poorer disease outcome.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-27T13:24:19.714Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bvd647g3mc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bvd647g3mc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Benjamin","last_name":"Krämer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rainer ","last_name":"Knoll","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lorenzo ","last_name":"Bonaguro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"ToVinh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jan ","last_name":"Raabe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna C. ","last_name":"Aschenbrenner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joachim L. ","last_name":"Schultze","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jacob ","last_name":"Nattermann","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Innate Immune Response","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g75gzdwbks","title":"Non-Invasive Monitoring of NK Cell Response in Cancer: A Focus on STAT1 Activation","description":"Our study presents imaging method that highlights the STAT1-mediated activation of NK cells to cancer cells. We engineered NK cells to incorporate an IFNγ activation sequence (GAS), which binds to the STAT1 transcription factor. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-12T09:17:12.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g75gzdwbks","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g75gzdwbks"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eun Hee","last_name":"Han","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Immunotherapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"62dtcgmfpf","title":"The combination of capecitabine and fecal microbiota transplantation in colorectal cancer treatment: reversing microbial dysbiosis and regulating the intestinal immune microenvironment","description":"The significant economic burden of colorectal cancer (CRC) necessitates the development of innovative therapeutic approaches. Interest in the gut microbiota's role in CRC has increased. Capecitabine, as a chemotherapy, may disrupt the balance of the intestinal microbiota. This study investigated the anticancer effects of capecitabine combined with fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) in a CRC mouse model caused by azoxymethane and dextran sodium sulfate. FMT was achieved with fecal microbiota from healthy mice through enema. Capecitabine decreased the number and diameter of cancer foci in CRC mice, while FMT supplementation had a more noticeable impact, indicated by increased body weight and survival rate. Capecitabine significantly reduced the abundance of pathogenic bacteria in mice with CRC, such as Bacteroides, Enterorhabdus, Monoglobus, Rodentibacter, uncultured_rumen_bacterium, Turicibacter, and Streptococcus. The supplementation of FMT more effectively reversed the gut microbiota dysbiosis in CRC mice, as demonstrated by the ACE and Chao 1 indices, PCoA analysis, and enhanced normal biological pathways. Microbial dysbiosis induced immunological dysfunction in CRC mice, indicated by abnormal immune cell recruitment and excessive cytokine production. Capecitabine treatment reduced immune cell infiltration, including CD3+ T cells, CD4+ T cells, and CD49b+ NK cells, as chemotherapy often suppresses the immune system. The supplement of FMT increased the proportion of CD4+ T cells, CD49b+ NK cells, CD8+ T cells, and LY6G+ neutrophils, indicating improved immune responses against CRC. Moreover, capecitabine therapy alone reduced the overexpression of IL1a, IL6, IL12a, IL12b, IL17, IL22, FOXP3, STAT3, IFN-γ, TNF-α, TGF-β, GZMA, CXCR4, OPN, PD-1 and PD-L1. FMT supplementation resulted in a higher immune response to CRC, as it had a greater inhibitory effect on the overexpression of inflammatory cytokines and enhanced the production of IL10, IFN-γ, and CXCR4. These cytokines were positively correlated with Azospirillum_sp._47_25, Romboutsia, Lactococcus, Rikenella_sp._Marseille_P3215 and Turicibacter and negatively correlated with Parabacteroids, unclassified_Oscillospiraceae, Marvinbryantia, unclassified_Clostridia_vadinBB60_group, unclassified_Erysipelatoclostridiaceae, A2, Roseburia, Rikenellaceae_RC9_gut_group, Acetatifactor and  unclassified_Clostridia. The combination of capecitabine and FMT is more effective at preventing CRC than capecitabine alone, as it reverses gut microbial abnormalities and boosts immune responses to CRC.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-06-16T23:34:36.864Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/62dtcgmfpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/62dtcgmfpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Muhammad","last_name":"Arshad","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chong-Yuan","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"占魁","last_name":"高","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hui","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"DanQi","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chao-Yuan","last_name":" Fan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bo-Wen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia-Xin","last_name":"Geng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aleksandr","last_name":"Kotusov","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ning","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shu-Lin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiao-Qin","last_name":"Mu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cytokines","Cancer Chemotherapy","Immune System","Colorectal Cancer","Gut Microbiota"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jsjh28yhfc","title":"Processed RNA-seq gene expression and enrichment analysis data from [Role of NK Cells in Immune Regulation and Sepsis-Induced Inflammation]","description":"Research Hypothesis and Data Description:\n\nThe dataset was generated to investigate the role of NK (natural killer) cells in immune regulation during sepsis-induced inflammation. We hypothesized that NK cells modulate the expression of key immune-related genes and pathways, affecting inflammation and tissue response during sepsis.\n\nData Summary:\n\nThe dataset consists of processed RNA-seq gene expression data from NK cells isolated from control and sepsis models.\n\nIt includes normalized gene expression matrices, lists of differentially expressed genes (DEGs), and functional enrichment analysis results (GO and KEGG pathways).\n\nVisualizations such as heatmaps, barplots, and volcano plots are also included to summarize gene expression patterns and enriched pathways.\n\nNotable Findings:\n\nNK cells in sepsis models show significant changes in genes involved in cytokine signaling, immune response, and inflammatory pathways.\n\nEnrichment analysis highlights key pathways potentially regulated by NK cells, such as TNF signaling, NF-κB pathway, and interferon-mediated responses.\nThe data suggests that NK cells may have both pro- and anti-inflammatory roles in sepsis progression, providing insights into immune modulation mechanisms.\n\nData Interpretation and Use:\n\nResearchers can use this dataset to explore NK cell-specific transcriptional changes, identify novel targets for immunomodulation, or perform comparative analysis with other immune cell types.\nThe processed gene expression and enrichment results allow reanalysis, integration with other datasets, and functional interpretation without needing access to raw sequencing files.\nThis dataset is particularly useful for studying immune regulation, inflammation, and cell-specific responses in sepsis models.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-30T16:33:49.279Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jsjh28yhfc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jsjh28yhfc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aneela","last_name":"Bashir","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Muhammad Asif","last_name":"Mrza","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Immunology","Life Sciences","Genomics","Bioinformatics","Transcriptomics","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxkp36zpss","title":"scRNAseq of CD45+ cells from Bladder Cancer Patient PBMC and Tumor and Healthy Donor NK Cells","description":"This dataset is associated with the manuscript https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.04.30.591366v2\n\n\nSample preparation for scRNASeq \nFor  BCa patients, live, CD45+ singlets were FACS-sorted (BD FACSAria II, 100µM nozzle, 4° C) from either Ficoll-isolated PBMC or freshly dissociated tumor tissue. Cells were sorted into DPBS + .04% BSA, resuspended at 1000 cells/µL, and loaded onto a Chromium controller (10x Genomics) for encapsulation into droplets, lysis, barcoding, reverse transcription, and library preparation using Single Cell 3' v2 reagents. For HD NK cells, PBMC were isolated as above, followed by depletion of non-NK lineages using immuno-magnetic beads (EasySep human NK cell enrichment kit, Stemcell Technologies). Samples were sequenced in dedicated flow cells on a HiSeq 2500 (Illumina) to an average depth of 91,787, 76,918, and 71,692 reads/cell for HD NK cells, BCa patient PBMC, and TIL respectively. \n\nPre-processing and analysis of scRNASeq \n10x Genomics 3' sequencing was performed. Raw Illumina sequencing data was converted to FASTQ, demultiplexed and aligned to the GRCh38 reference genome using CellRanger v3.0.0 (10x Genomics).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-17T16:02:57.243Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxkp36zpss","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxkp36zpss"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Adam","last_name":"Farkas","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01 CA249175 "},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01 CA201189 "},{"id":"https://ror.org/0447fe631","name":"United States Department of Defense","grant_id":"CA181008"}],"categories":["Immunology","Tumor Immunology","Bladder Cancer","Natural Killer Cell"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":93773,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":125,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":93773,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":93534,"restricted":46,"embargoed":193},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1596},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":554},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":381},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":326},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":318},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":316},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":295},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":275},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":207},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":168}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":91597},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1597},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":169},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":93},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":59},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":57},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":42},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":31},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":28},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":385},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":372},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":346},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":229},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":223}]}}